  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>The RICS Home Survey Standard defines three survey levels: Level 1 condition report, Level 2 homebuyer survey, Level 3 building survey</li><li>Building elements are given condition ratings: 1 (no repair needed), 2 (defects to attend to, not urgent), 3 (serious or urgent)</li><li>Capturing the rating and a photo against each element on site produces a better and more defensible report than a desk write-up</li><li>A dated, timestamped photo per element is the record that defends the surveyor in a professional indemnity dispute</li><li>Charging 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, beats per-seat for firms with a mix of field and office staff, because you only pay for who works</li><li>The app captures the survey and speeds the write-up; it does not replace the chartered surveyor or a specific RICS template</li></ul></div><h2>What a building survey report app is for</h2><p>A building survey is a walk through a property, element by element, with a trained eye: roof, walls, windows, services, damp and movement. The surveyor forms a view on each, gives it a condition rating, and photographs the evidence. The write-up that follows is where surveyor time drains away.</p><p>The app does not make the survey valid. The chartered surveyor does. What it does is make the record easier to complete on site, harder to miss an element, and far faster to turn into a report. The best report is the one where the ratings and photos were captured in the room, not stitched together at the desk from a camera roll.</p><h2>The RICS Home Survey Standard and its three levels</h2><p>The RICS Home Survey Standard, mandatory for RICS members since 2021, sets a consistent framework. Level 1 is a condition report with ratings and limited advice. Level 2 is the homebuyer survey, with or without a valuation, with more explanation of defects. Level 3 is the building survey, the most thorough inspection, suited to older, larger or altered homes.</p><p>Quickler runs the level as a workflow: the surveyor picks the level and the prompts and depth follow from it. It is a capture and reporting aid, not a certified RICS template, and it does not decide the level or ratings for you.</p><h2>Condition ratings 1, 2 and 3</h2><p>Rating 1 means no repair is presently needed. Rating 2 means a defect that needs attention but is not serious or urgent. Rating 3 means a defect that is serious or needs urgent attention or investigation. Clients and solicitors read the ratings first, so they must be assigned against each element at the point of observation, with a supporting photo.</p><p>Quickler captures the rating and photo element by element and flags rating 3 items for the office as they are entered. It records your rating; it does not choose it for you.</p><h2>On site versus office: capture in the room</h2><p>The evening write-up is where surveys lose their edge. Photos get mislabelled, small defects drop out, and the surveyor half-remembers which crack was where. A rating captured in the room, with the element in front of you, is more accurate and carries more conviction than one assigned days later.</p><p>Quickler lets the surveyor dictate each element as a voice note or a line of text and attach the photo there and then. You leave with the survey substantially recorded. It does not replace your judgement about what a defect means; it captures it while the evidence is in front of you.</p><h2>The photo record that defends the rating</h2><p>Building surveys generate professional indemnity claims when a buyer later says a defect was missed or under-rated. The defence is the record: what was inspected, what was found, how it was rated, and a dated photograph to prove it. Quickler stamps every photo with the time it was taken and the element it belongs to, and keeps the survey as an ordered evidence trail. That supports a defensible file, but it is not a substitute for competent inspection or adequate cover, and it is not legal advice. Check your PI terms and current RICS guidance.</p><h2>What to look for in a building survey report app</h2><ul><li><strong>Level-aware workflow:</strong> the prompts and depth should follow the survey level, not force one rigid form on all three</li><li><strong>Ratings and photos element by element:</strong> each rating tied to a timestamped image at the point of observation</li><li><strong>Rating 3 visibility:</strong> serious or urgent findings surfaced to the office as they are entered, not when the PDF lands</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes firms for office staff and associates. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, so you only pay for who works</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> surveyor, property, date and every element and rating in sequence, for the PI dispute that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the ratings, findings and photos and speeds the write-up. It does not make a survey valid, and it is not a certified RICS report template. The chartered surveyor's competence and judgement carry the validity. Follow current RICS guidance and treat this as general information, not legal advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a building survey report app?</h3><p>It is a tool that helps a surveyor record a RICS building or condition survey on site and produce the report: capturing the survey level, condition ratings and photos element by element. Options range from paper and dictation, to generic audit apps, to conversation-based tools like quickler that run the survey over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>What are the RICS survey levels and condition ratings?</h3><p>The RICS Home Survey Standard sets three levels: Level 1 (condition report), Level 2 (homebuyer survey, with or without valuation) and Level 3 (building survey). Building elements are rated 1 (no repair presently needed), 2 (defect needing attention but not urgent) or 3 (serious or urgent defect). Always follow current RICS guidance for the detail of each level and rating.</p><h3>Does the app replace a RICS report template?</h3><p>No. Quickler captures the ratings, findings and photos on site and speeds the write-up, but it is not a certified RICS report template and it does not make the survey valid. The chartered surveyor's competence and judgement do that. Follow current RICS guidance and your firm's own report format, and treat this as general information, not legal or professional advice.</p><h3>Can I do a building survey over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's survey workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The surveyor receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, records the rating and photo for each element with text, a voice note or an image, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/surveyor-site-report-software-uk.html\">Surveyor site report software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/snagging-inspection-report-app-uk.html\">Snagging inspection report app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dilapidations-schedule-of-condition-uk.html\">Schedule of condition and dilapidations</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/structural-engineer-report-software-uk.html\">Structural engineer report software</a></li></ul>",
